{"reply":"You can acknowledge the effort without treating it as a reason to give up your voice. For example:

> “I recognize that you’ve been carrying a lot and putting in effort to move this forward. I appreciate that. I also want to be honest that ending the planning meetings and reversing the plan left me feeling out of the loop. Could we make space for the team to discuss major changes before they’re finalized? I’m not questioning your character or commitment—I’m asking for a clearer way to have a say in decisions that affect our work.”

Keeping the focus on specific actions, their impact, and the process you’re requesting helps you speak up without judging who they are. You can respect their burden and still ask for shared decision-making.","memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

